{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 38, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from the year 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a tumultuous era marked by rising Islamic power under Saladin. His life story involves complex political maneuvering alongside significant military engagements that shaped the fate of the Crusader states in the Levant. Raymond inherited his title after his father died while imprisoned and he spent his early years consolidating power within his own county before expanding his influence into the broader kingdom affairs. He became known for his pragmatic approach to diplomacy and his willingness to negotiate with Muslim leaders when necessary to preserve Christian holdings.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond faced immediate challenges upon assuming control because his father Raymond II had been assassinated inside the cathedral of Tortosa by members of the Order of Assassins. This tragic event left the young count vulnerable to external threats from neighboring Muslim powers and internal strife among the nobility. He managed to secure his position through strategic alliances and by demonstrating capable leadership despite his youth. His marriage to Eschiva of Bures brought him additional lands and strengthened his claim to influence within the kingdom hierarchy.\n\n*Regency and Political Conflict*\nThe count served as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not rule independently during certain periods of his reign. Raymond acted as guardian for the young king and attempted to maintain stability while factions within the court plotted against each other. He opposed the aggressive policies of Guy of Lusignan and supported alternative succession plans involving Raymond of Toron or even the sister of the king. These political struggles weakened the unity of the Crusader states right before the catastrophic Battle of Hattin occurred in 1187.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Legacy*\nRaymond commanded forces during several critical battles including the disastrous engagement at Hattin where the Crusader army was destroyed by Saladin. He survived this defeat by breaking through enemy lines with a small group of knights while many others were captured or killed. Following the loss of Jerusalem he continued to defend Tripoli and negotiated a separate peace treaty with Saladin to save his own county from total destruction. Historians debate whether his actions constituted treason or prudent statecraft given the impossible circumstances facing the Latin East. His death later that same year marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli and removed a major voice of moderation from the region.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -2.8253e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0024328595}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0067519285}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -2.7449958}, {"token": " year", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.0728842e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -1.1920936e-06}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -2.7418175e-06}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -6.4373226e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -2.5033983e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -3.6954948e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.000807671}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.00013256952}, {"token": " played", 